read: app:doctrine-church~9125 | As His custom was: Jesus went to church
read: app:doctrine-church~15215 | Jotham entered not into the temple
read: app:doctrine-church~16492 | Mine house is waste

=== Christ the foundation
# from 12564, 5266
--- Upon this rock
kicker: Christ the foundation
scripture: Matthew 16:15-18 | mark: Thou art the Christ, the Son of the living God; upon this rock I will build my church
p: Jesus, in confirming Peter's profession of faith, that Jesus is the Christ, the Son of God, says that this truth revealed to Peter by God, and to all of us through His word and received by faith, is the rock upon which Jesus is building his church.
p: Cephas or Peter, which translated means a stone ([[John 1:42]]). It is this confession of the faith that Jesus says the gates of Hell shall not prevail against.

# from 14406, 17969
--- Lively stones
kicker: Christ the foundation
big: 1 Corinthians 3:11 | mark: other foundation can no man lay than that is laid, which is Jesus Christ
big: 1 Peter 2:5 | mark: Ye also, as lively stones, are built up a spiritual house, an holy priesthood
p: We who believe, as Peter says in his Epistles, are as lively stones like him, making up the edification, the building up of the church.

# from 10435, 14435, 13843
--- We are the tabernacle
kicker: Christ the foundation
lead: In Christ, we are the Tabernacle, we are the temple. Our bodies the house of the Lord.
p: Jesus IS the Temple, the Lord God Almighty is the Temple. The church built upon the truth of the Gospel, that Jesus is Christ, are the pillars of that Temple.
p: Jesus left us the Gospel. God's word. He builds His church from the pillars that are saved believers. Adopted sons of God.
refs: 1 Corinthians 3:16; Revelation 3:12; Revelation 21:22
read: app:doctrine-church~12564 | Upon this rock: Matthew 16
read: app:doctrine-church~14406 | Upon this rock I will build my church
read: heresies-that-rock-was-christ | That Rock Was Christ, Not Peter

=== Local, independent churches
# from 972, 1238
--- Local, independent, Jesus-led
kicker: Local, independent churches
lead: Biblical churches planted by the Apostles were local, independent, Jesus-led, via a pastor aka a bishop.
p: Elders of the church sent evangelists out to start new independent churches, and members were sent out as the priesthood of believers to win souls locally. They had one Gospel, the doctrine came directly from scripture and had no hidden or extra-biblical offices, rites or oral tradition.
p: Yes, churches is plural. That's the NT Christian perspective on a church. A fellowship of like-minded churches. Not a universal church on earth.
refs: Romans 16:16; Galatians 1:2; Revelation 1:11

# from 8726, 14827
--- No top-down denominations
kicker: Local, independent churches
lead: There is no biblical basis for top down denominational structure of a church.
p: Large institutions of men where power is consolidated always will drift into apostasy, and when it does it takes down huge swaths of people. An independent church might falter or pick up a strange doctrine, but the people within can leave and often just go across town to another independent Bible believing church.
tile: I | independent of any denomination
tile: F | fundamentalist by inclination and practice
tile: B | Baptist because they agree on the first two
take: It's amazing how God's system for churches is so much superior to man's.

# from 15406, 15407, 7811
--- The candlesticks are the churches
kicker: Local, independent churches
big: Revelation 1:20 | mark: the seven candlesticks which thou sawest are the seven churches
p: These are literal places and people, real actual churches, and the lessons given are to serve as a model for our churches today. Even the worst had members who were right with God.
p: When faced with a lukewarm church, the answer isn't to become cold to church and drop out, it is to become red hot for the Lord and heat things up. Zeal is contagious.
refs: Revelation 2:4-5; Revelation 3:15-16

# from 17331
--- The churches the Apostles planted
kicker: Local, independent churches
lead: My church traces its doctrine directly back to the Apostles, who founded local independent churches.
p: In Jerusalem, Antioch, Athens, Berea, Caesarea, Cenchrea, Colossae, Corinth, Crete, Damascus, Derbe, Ephesus, Iconium, Joppa, Laodicea, Lystra, Pergamum, Philadelphia, Philippi, Rome, Sardis, Smyrna, Thessalonica, Thyatira, and Troas...
p: My pastor has been trained directly by the Apostles, to uphold the same traditions those first century churches held to, as God documents in His scriptures.
read: app:doctrine-church~972 | The New Testament church: local, independent and Jesus-led
read: app:doctrine-church~8726 | No Top-Down Denominations
read: app:Revelation-2 | The churches of Revelation 2 and 3
read: doctrine-week-5-trail-of-blood-and-baptist-history | Week 5: A Trail of Blood and Being Baptist

=== Pastors and deacons
# from 5768, 2572
--- Two offices
kicker: Pastors and deacons
lead: The only two biblical church offices; Bishop and Deacon. Bishop being the pastor, or head of a local independent NT church, and deacon being an assistive role, aiding the pastor in the administration of the church.
scripture: Acts 6:3-4 | mark: seven men of honest report, full of the Holy Ghost and wisdom; we will give ourselves continually to prayer, and to the ministry of the word
p: They should be Christ-led, pastor in charge, deacons serving the pastor, as the Bible lays out.

# from 11326, 5803
--- The qualifications of a bishop
kicker: Pastors and deacons
scripture: 1 Timothy 3:2-7 | mark: A bishop then must be blameless, the husband of one wife
p: Not that he, his wife or kids must be perfect, but that the pastors and deacons be in control at home.
p: Telegram isn't church. Discord isn't church. I am not a pastor. Unless someone leads an actual assembly of believers, in real life, they are not a pastor.

# from 15610, 3926, 1238
--- A royal priesthood
kicker: Pastors and deacons
big: 1 Peter 2:9 | mark: a royal priesthood
p: Every saved believer is of the priesthood of Christ. Giving the gospel that others become part of that holy priesthood of believers. There is *one mediator between God and men, the man Christ Jesus* ([[1 Timothy 2:5]]).
p: A saved woman is called to give the gospel and save souls and use their gifts to the edification of the church, just as a man is. But in church, under the leadership of a man of God, the pastor, they are to learn in silence. It isn't the role but the goal.
refs: 1 Timothy 2:12; Romans 16:1-2

# from 16748, 5853
--- The labourer is worthy of his reward
kicker: Pastors and deacons
big: 1 Timothy 5:17-18 | mark: The labourer is worthy of his reward
p: It's a pretty clear line between being paid for the ministry with enough to live on, and taking advantage of the position to become rich from it. Tithes and supporting a pastor are a good thing. Pastors are to not take advantage of their flock.
p: And accusations against an elder should be handled with respect, requiring witnesses, but publicly rebuked if they don't repent. By not being respecters of persons in these matters, an active lively church can avoid these pitfalls.
read: app:doctrine-church~5803 | Qualifications of bishop and deacon
read: app:doctrine-church~2572 | Acts 6: the office of deacon
read: app:doctrine-church~11326 | Telegram isn't church
read: app:doctrine-church~16748 | A pastor is worthy of his hire

=== One body, two ordinances
# from 12798, 12817
--- Baptized into one body
kicker: One body, two ordinances
big: 1 Corinthians 12:13 | mark: by one Spirit are we all baptized into one body
p: By the Spirit of God that has baptized us into the body of Christ, a spiritual baptism, we become partakers of the gifts given every man, according to the purpose of God. Christ as the head.
p: These passages are not about the gifts, but about the hearts of Christians regarding them. That we all work as one body, under the head that is Christ.

# from 3699, 13156
--- A little leaven
kicker: One body, two ordinances
big: 1 Corinthians 5:6-7 | mark: a little leaven leaveneth the whole lump
p: Jesus taught of church discipline in **Matthew 18**, first alone, then with witness, then before the entire church if the person won't take the reproach. Far too many churches today, even doctrinally sound ones full of believers, fail at this simple task.
p: And when a brother or sister in Christ comes into your congregation, their past bondage should not be held against them. Their sins are as forgiven as yours. *Thou shalt not oppress him.*
refs: Matthew 18:15-17; Deuteronomy 23:15-16

# from 9192, 4876
--- Believer's baptism
kicker: One body, two ordinances
lead: There are two new ordinances made to which we should adhere. Baptism and the Lord's supper. Just as the ordinances of the OT held no actual power to forgive sin or earn grace, but were a picture of the Christ to come, so too the ordinances we are told to keep are an image of Christ.
p: In baptism, the believer is immersed in water, dead, buried and risen in the manner reminiscent of Jesus. Baptism not being the putting away of sin, but the answer of a good conscience by those who have called upon the name of the Lord.
big: 1 Peter 3:21 | mark: not the putting away of the filth of the flesh, but the answer of a good conscience toward God
refs: Acts 8:36-38; Romans 6:4

# from 9754
--- This do in remembrance of me
kicker: One body, two ordinances
big: Luke 22:19 | mark: this do in remembrance of me
p: The Lord's supper is a continuation of Passover, which pictured Jesus. Jesus is that lamb, who takes away the sins of the world. The bread now, represents His body. The cup poured out, His blood. It is a memorial and a picture of Christ.
p: Done within the congregation of believers, using the breaking of unleavened bread and the pouring out of the unfermented juice of the vine, done in memory of Jesus. It is not a means of obtaining grace or salvation.
read: app:doctrine-church~12798 | Baptized by one Spirit into one body
read: app:doctrine-church~3699 | A little leaven leaveneth the whole lump
read: app:doctrine-church~9192 | Baptism and the Lord's supper are pictures
read: app:doctrine-church~9754 | The Lord's supper is a memorial, not a sacrament

=== Assembling together
# from 16571, 8691
--- Not forsaking the assembling
kicker: Assembling together
scripture: Hebrews 10:23-25 | mark: Not forsaking the assembling of ourselves together
p: This is an appeal to go to church. Note, not for salvation, not just because it's sin not to, not because you can lose your salvation if you don't. But like all good works, to the benefit of others and a reflection of the love God has shown us in our salvation.
p: This appeal is explicitly about what we should do because things are getting worse, not an excuse not to.

# from 11313, 11314
--- White-knuckle Christianity
kicker: Assembling together
lead: For many Christians, they are living what can only be described as “white-knuckle Christianity.”
p: They are trying to get right without any guidance. Without accountability. Without a church. Without real life brethren to hold them accountable. Without guidance from older and more mature Christians that know them personally.
p: They are easily taken in by people who seem to have more knowledge. People that they don't know personally. And they tend to be carried away by strange doctrines.

# from 16743, 15404
--- Many hands make light work
kicker: Assembling together
lead: All hands on deck is how we should approach the local church. If you have a talent or skill or even time, you should pitch in.
p: It is where the works of God are done. Edifying others, praying as a group, singing Psalms and hymns, giving one to another, learning under a pastor, and most importantly, soul winning and evangelizing of some kind, even if it's just invitations and tracts.
refs: Nehemiah 4:6; Ephesians 4:11-12; Colossians 3:16

# from 15442
--- Bring them to church
kicker: Assembling together
lead: Every saved believer has a difficult time preaching to their loved ones, and getting friends and relatives saved.
big: Mark 6:4 | mark: A prophet is not without honour, but in his own country
p: If the Lord Jesus Christ had a hard time reaching those who knew Him best, it is natural that we do too. But church-goers? Where the word is preached? There is a solution: Inviting them to services. Christmas, Easter, potluck, perhaps your own baptism, or just because.
read: app:doctrine-church~16571 | Assembling together and not falling away
read: app:doctrine-church~11313 | White-knuckle Christianity
read: app:doctrine-church~16743 | Many hands make light work
